From 4da0b56903bfeceea758acaf6308bd4ba54eacb4 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Connor Baker <ConnorBaker01@gmail.com>
Date: Mon, 28 Sep 2026 08:34:50 +0000
Subject: [PATCH 3/4] musl: implement gettid(), raise() and abort() with system
 calls

musl caches the calling thread's TID in its thread descriptor, and only
its own fork() and pthread_create() update it. In a child created with
raw_clone() it is the parent's TID, and every musl function that uses
it acts on the parent's thread:

- gettid() returns the parent's TID, so is_main_thread() is false in the
  child's only thread, and e.g. rename_process() fails with EPERM
  ("Failed to rename process, ignoring: Operation not permitted" from
  children of PID 1).
- raise() sends the signal to the parent's thread (tkill on the cached
  TID) and returns 0; the child carries on. safe_fork_full() calls it in
  a child made with raw_clone() (FORK_NEW_*NS) when the parent died
  early.
- abort() raises SIGABRT the same way, so a failed assertion in such a
  child (log_assert_failed(), assert_raw(), mmap-cache's abort()) sends
  SIGABRT to its parent, before the child crashes itself.

Implement all three in the musl compatibility layer with the system
calls: gettid() with gettid(2), raise() with tgkill(getpid(), gettid(),
sig) with all signals blocked (as musl's raise() blocks them), and
abort() through that raise(), then, as musl's abort() does, with SIGABRT
reset to its default action and unblocked, and failing that SIGKILL and
_exit(127).

This covers systemd's own calls, which the headers in src/include/musl/
redirect. It does not cover calls compiled elsewhere: inside musl (its
assert(), via __assert_fail(), and anything else in libc that calls its
internal raise() or abort()) and in the libraries linked in statically
(e.g. util-linux's libblkid calls abort()); nor musl's other uses of the
cached TID:
pthread_kill(pthread_self(), ...) and the owner of its stdio and mutex
locks. systemd's own assertions do not go through <assert.h>.

A reproducer: a static musl program whose clone(SIGCHLD) child calls
raise(SIGUSR1) or abort(). Before, the parent receives SIGUSR1 (the
child exits 0), or SIGABRT (the child then dies of SIGSEGV); with this
layer, as with glibc, the child dies of SIGUSR1 or SIGABRT and the
parent receives nothing.

